A||||11||rockcrawl||General sway bar question||||5||
Z||000000||rockcrawl||09-15-2000||10:56 PM||||OK, Most cars on the road have the engine up front. They also have a large swaw bar up front, and a little or no rear bar. So wouldn't it make sense, when moving the engine to the rear, to move the larger or only sway bar to the rear also? I'm sure I'm wrong, or pontiac would have done it, but can someone explain why?<P>My reason for asking; I'm using the front engine/cradle/suspension from a Cadillac in the rear of my Ferrari replica, and as a bonus I get a wide track, big brakes and a 1-1/8" sway bar. I also have a 1" bar for the front. How will the bigger rear bar affect handling?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fer3_1.jpg"> <P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||09-15-2000||11:36 PM||||I can't think of a single car that has a bigger rear bar than the front..... That should mean something....<P>I'm not sure what it will do to handling but it likely aint good.<P>The bigger bar works in front for allot of the same reason you have bigger brakes in front than rear... weight transfers onto the outside front corner in a turn. Even with the mid engine the outside front is heavier than the rear when turning.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||09-16-2000||12:54 AM||||Umm... No.<BR>Your vehicle dynamics are wrong. The outside front would be haevier than the outside rear only if you are braking in a turn. For steady state cornering (which hardly ever happens), The normal static f/r weight distribution applies, and is then modified by anti-swaybars.<P>The reason most cars have large front bars is to induce understeer and make them safe for the average (i.e. incompetent) driver.<BR>Herb Adams recommends 1 - 1 1/8" front bar, with a 1 1/4 (!) inch rear bar.<BR>However, only skilled drivers with an otherwise excellent chassis setup can get away with that one.<P>Chassis setup is much too complicated to go into here, but if you are building a Ferrari, you should definitely know about it if you want to build the car RIGHT.<BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.motorbooks.com," TARGET=_blank>www.motorbooks.com,</A>  search for "suspension", and take your pick of what it returns.<BR>I recommend Chassis Engineering by Herb Adams as a good place to start learning about vehicle dynamics.||||reg||1
Z||000003||gixxer||09-16-2000||01:04 AM||||A sway bar has two uses.<BR>One is to keep the body roll from being excessive, which helps keep the tire footprint square to the road.<BR>The other use is to tune the handling by changing the vertical loading (force pressing the tire to the pavement) of the tires, which will change the traction at that end of the car.<BR>Tires are sensitive to vertical load. A tire will corner better with more vertical load, to a point. But the cornering improvements will diminish then start to drop if the vertical loads become too great(that's why bigger, heavier cars need bigger tires). <BR>A sway bar works by transferring the load from the inside tire at one end of the car, to the outside tire. This effectively tends to remove weight from the inside tire, and overloads (relatively) the outer tire. <BR>So if you were to experiment with a pair of tires at one end of a car, without a sway bar, and with a sway bar, you'd find that the pair of tires WITHOUT the sway bar will generate higher cornering forces, as the tires are more evenly loaded (This is assuming that body roll is kept under control, and the tire contact patch orientation is reasonable).<BR>So what pretty much ALL rear drive cars will have is a bar in the front, which will load up the outside front, generally causing understeer. No bar on the rear suspension frees it up to develop maximum traction (more understeer) and lets the inside rear tire stay planted on the ground while accelerating out of a corner. <BR>If a bar were to be put on the rear, on a rear drive car, in a corner, it would be removing weight from the inside rear tire, transfering it to the outside rear, thus removing some of the cornering power from the rear tire pair (more of an oversteer condition, or less understeer), and also potentially hurting some of the traction from the inside rear coming off the corner. <BR>So once the springs and swaybar(s) are JUST stiff enough to keep the body roll under control, the sway bars stiffness can be used to balance a cars' handling by removing traction from the front or from the rear. <BR>If you check out some older pictures of Formula 1 cars or rear engined Porsches, you may see the inside front being lifted clear off the ground in a corner. A stiff front sway bar is being used to keep the car level so that the softest spring/swaybar settings  can be used at the rear giving the best traction possible to the rear driving tires.<BR>Also consider front drive cars lifting the inside rears so that the front tires can develop maximum traction.<BR>Cheers, Brian     ||||reg||1
Z||000004||gtsonly||09-16-2000||05:56 AM||||so, is it a good idea to add a rear swaybar to a stock fiero or not? or are you saying it will hinder performance?<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPS<BR>http://www.fiero.nl/cgi-bin/fiero/ubbmisc.cgi||||reg||5
Z||000005||rockcrawl||09-16-2000||08:41 AM||||Thanks for the info so far guys! It looks like I'm going to have to go back to school just to set up my car. The mental picture of a porsche with the inside front tire in the air helps explain things a lot.<BR> My chassis setup is far less than excellent I'm sure (Cadi front control arms with coil-overs on the rear, and stock early Fiero front). I really don't plan on doing any extreme driving with the car, but I don't want it to be unsafe. I really only need the swaybars to keep body roll to a minimum, not so much for tuning. So it's either leave the 1-1/8" bar on the rear or take it off, I'm not going to play around with sizing. I like the way my 86 GT handles with a rear bar, it seems to work really well.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000006||gixxer||09-16-2000||12:59 PM||||Rockcrawl, I can't tell you what you're gonna need. You'll have to use your best judgement. I'd drive the car first, carefully with the big bar and see what it feels like. Remember, you can use poly bushings for the front bar, a little positive camber, change tire size, wheel width, etc. to make the front end understeer, and vice versa. Keep in mind 'tho that it's generally better to add traction if possible, to balance a cars handling, than to reduce traction.  <BR>Keep in mind that my little dissertation, while accurate enough, was in compressed sound bite form. I didn't even mention tire slip angles! Read even the slimmest book on car handling to get a better overall feel for what's going on.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Ed||09-16-2000||03:23 PM||||For gtsonly:<P>The Fiero was designed with a lot of understeer built in (remember the Corvair?, GM did and I do too, I had one).  <P>I've put rear bars on both my Fieros.  That reduces the understeer and makes them generally handle better.  Some of the above discussion was pretty detailed because of context, but for most common street uses, rear bars help. ||||reg||1
