Z||000010||myblack86GT||09-16-2000||10:36 PM||||Oh, okay, Those look very close to the stock Ferrari. I really like those. I am planning on a complete teardown and rebuild of an 84 or 85 Fiero. Adding a body kit and a V8 SBC. I noticed you said your are using an engine/cradle/suspension from a Caddy. Would it be possible, or difficult to put the caddy Suspension on my car, without useing the cradle or engine? Just wondering because I looked into the Northstar engine, but cant afford it because I have a budget for this car, so I decided to use Archies SBC engine instead. What do those Caddy engines cost anyway? The only place I looked was a place called Design One I think. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000011||rockcrawl||09-17-2000||08:07 PM||||myblack86GT,<BR> I was going to email you directly to keep this post from going too far off topic, but your address is unavailable. <P> The wheels do look kinda Ferrar-ish. The Cadi suspension would be nearly impossible without using the cradle, and probably not any better performing than the stock fiero parts. It's much the same, only bigger and wider. The Northstar is beyond my budget as well. I got a wrecked 91 Seville with 4.9 liter SFI V8 for $350. The engine makes 200 hp and 275 lb/ft torque, and weighs only 10 lb more than a 2.8. It can be swapped into a Fiero with much the same efforts as a 3800 swap. I only used the cadi cradle to get the wider trac and bigger brakes, etc.. I'm considering swapping out the 4spd auto for a 5 spd manual, but still undecided. If you're considering a V8 swap, look into the 4.9, It is cheaper and better suited for the fiero than a SBC, unless you need LOTS of hp at the expense of added weight.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'86 GT 3800 (for sale)<BR>'87 GT (noisey 2.8)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
